WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.680
- [Narrator] If you want to conduct

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.240
experiments like this in space,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.380
- We are trying to grow edible plants.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.600
- [Narrator] this is
the only place to do it.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.600
But soon, the International
Space Station will have company.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.290
In April, China launched the core part

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.640
of its new space station, called Tiangong,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.951
which means heavenly palace in Chinese.

00:00:19.951 --> 00:00:23.500
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.290
- This is a brand new
modular space station.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.260
There are many exciting things about it.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.360
- [Narrator] China says Tiangong

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.500
will be up and running by 2022,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.100
becoming a research spacecraft
that will orbit Earth

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.360
alongside the nearly 23-year-old

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.070
International Space Station.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.900
NASA and its partner countries have agreed

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.070
that the older spacecraft
will operate through 2024,

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.200
though its future after that is unclear.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.990
- If the International Space
Station retires in 2024,

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:49.823
the Chinese space station

00:00:49.823 --> 00:00:53.850
will be the only permanent space
station in low Earth orbit.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.340
It means that China
could have a very strong

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.380
global presence and influence.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.650
- [Narrator] So here's what's
on China's new space station

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.000
and how it compares with
the international one.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.300
The first thing you have to know

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.830
about China's space station,
it's building it all by itself,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.680
and some of the reasons for
that go back about a decade.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.810
- China is not part of the
International Space Station.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:17.970
- [Narrator] Quentin
Parker is the Director

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.820
of the Laboratory for Space Research

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.740
at the University of Hong Kong.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.740
He's been researching space
science for over four decades.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.760
- They're primarily political

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.980
around issues of trust
and technology transfer,

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.780
which is driven principally
by the United States.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.580
- [Narrator] Since 2011, China
has been excluded by U.S. law

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.030
from working with NASA.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.330
As a result, China's astronauts
have not been allowed

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.760
to set foot on the
International Space Station,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.920
which is a collaboration
with five space agencies.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.950
China's foreign ministry
said that the U.S.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.800
is judging an upright
man with a petty mind.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.980
- In some ways, it's been
extremely beneficial to China,

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.040
because it's given China the impetus

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.310
and the political will to move forward

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:00.183
with their own very
ambitious space endeavors.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.880
- [Narrator] And China's
space station ambition

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.693
has been 30 years in the making.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.370
In 2003, it's sent its
first astronauts to space,

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:11.640
making it the third country

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.693
after the U.S. and Russia to do that.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:15.850
Less than a decade later,

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.970
China said it successfully
launched two test modules

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.630
which are the basis for
any space station design.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.160
- These modules are then put
together a bit like Lego.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.047
The reason in modular, of course,

00:02:25.047 --> 00:02:27.230
is you can't put the
whole thing up in one go.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.780
You have to put it up bit
by bit using many launches.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.090
- [Narrator] The
International Space Station

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.110
currently has 16 modules,

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.490
whereas China only has one in orbit.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.180
But eventually, Tiangong
will be made up of three.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.230
- It's only about a quarter or so

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.790
of the size of the current
International Space Station.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:44.770
Three people can live on

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:45.987
the Chinese space station at the moment.

00:02:45.987 --> 00:02:47.790
- [Narrator] Compare
that with the six people

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.410
usually on board the
International Space Station,

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.850
or in 2009 when there was a record total

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:54.960
of 13 astronauts at one time.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.840
And China could potentially
reach that capacity too,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.930
just by adding more modules.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:02.620
Thanks to this design, the
International Space Station

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.500
has been an evolving project since 1998.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.920
- So if one module has
failed catastrophically,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.990
or it's no longer cutting edge,

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.093
you can detach it and
just reattach a new one.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.070
- [Narrator] While the
Tiangong spacecraft is smaller,

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.940
China says it's equipped with
more advanced technology.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:21.576
- When you're the last kid on the block,

00:03:21.576 --> 00:03:23.180
do you have the opportunity to do things

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.030
at the last minute with
the best technology around.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.110
- [Narrator] And one of them is a system

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.716
that helps power Tiangong
with solar panels.

00:03:29.716 --> 00:03:33.633
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.970
The electricity generated
from the panel wings

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.970
essentially helps keep the lights on.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.060
But one unique part about China's setup

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.210
is that it also powers

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.540
the space craft's propulsion technology.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:49.600
So even though Tiangong still
uses regular fuel propellants

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.310
like the International Space Station,

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.380
China says it doesn't have to use as much

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.030
to stay afloat in orbit.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.470
- If you're not using a liquid
or solid fuel propellant

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.030
for your propulsion, then
you're not carrying that weight,

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:02.810
then that's a saving.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:03.860
- [Narrator] China's space agency

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.190
says its propulsion system

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.480
is about five times more
efficient and powerful

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.540
than the International Space Station's,

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:10.890
which is a gas guzzler.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.060
- So we're gonna fire the engines

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.920
in the aft end of the space station.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.090
- [Narrator] Consuming about
nine tons of fuel annually

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:18.850
to stay in orbit.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:19.920
Another piece of equipment

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.240
that the International
Space Station doesn't have,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.050
a tag-a-long space telescope.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:25.090
It's called Xuntian,

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.260
which means heavenly cruiser in Chinese,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.510
and will orbit parallel
to the Space Station.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.660
- A bit like the Hubble Space Telescope,

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.210
but this will have an
enormously large camera

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.290
which can look up to 300
times the field of view

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.360
of the Hubble Space Telescope in one hit.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.490
- [Narrator] The Chinese
telescope will be able to refuel

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:45.420
and be serviced by directly
docking at Tiangong,

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:46.800
unlike the Hubble Telescope,

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.380
which because it orbits on its own,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.850
requires astronauts to fly
in from Earth for repairs.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:53.580
And one day, China space telescope

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.260
could be used by other countries,

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.360
because China has said
it's new space station

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.280
is designed for collaborations.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.100
- So for China, international
cooperation in space

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.530
means its space capacity has legitimacy,

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:08.080
and second, it can build influence.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.420
- [Narrator] Namrata Goswami
is an independent researcher

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.230
who's been studying global space policy

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:13.650
for more than 20 years.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.880
- [Namrata] With the completion
of the Chinese space station

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.720
by 2022, that will be
a competitive alignment

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:23.120
for countries that want to choose

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.830
which space station they would
launch their experiments on.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.240
- [Narrator] Goswami says
China's decision-making process

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.170
could be very different

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.070
from the International Space Station,

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:35.570
which requires consensus
among its partners.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:38.610
- Chinese space station is
going to be operated by China.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:43.610
Any decision to allow a
particular scientific experiment

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.270
to be conducted in the
Chinese space station

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.618
will of course be taken by China.

00:05:49.618 --> 00:05:53.535
(speaking in foreign language)

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.790
- [Narrator] And more of these
decisions could come soon,

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:05.278
since Tiangong is set
to be completed by 2022.

00:06:05.278 --> 00:06:09.195
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.920
And that milestone is another
one in just a few years,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.770
in addition to landing on
the far side of the Moon,

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.850
to sending a rover to roam Mars.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:22.070
- It is very clear that
China's investment in space

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:25.210
is about turning China
into a aerospace power.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:26.680
- [Narrator] But China
won't be the only one

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:27.910
left to fill the vacancy

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:31.250
when the International Space
Station heads into retirement.

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.410
NASA is leading a plan
to launch an outpost

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.580
that will work like the
International Space Station

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.700
but instead orbit the Moon.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:38.810
And Russia has indicated

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.810
that it could launch its
own space station by 2030.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.940
- Space is always seen
as a part of geopolitics.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.370
The competition that is ongoing

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:51.720
is which country has the more
attractive space capacity

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.523
in low Earth orbit and beyond?

